{% comment %}
Full screen, 800x480.

One structure serves one, two and three printers, and only the headline size
changes. The alternative was a separate hero layout for the single-printer case,
which is how the first attempt clipped a 170px glyph inside a 143px flex row:
`.layout` is `overflow: hidden`, and `layout--stretch` divides the height
equally whether or not the contents fit.

The content area is about 430px once the title bar is out. So the headline is
220px for one printer, 74px for two and 58px for three, each leaving room for
the rail and the supporting figures.

Every metric is guarded. An absent value leaves nothing behind rather than
becoming a zero, which is why an idle printer has no progress rail at all
rather than an empty one.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Two headline sizes, not one. A percentage is three or four narrow glyphs; a
  state word is eight wide ones, and at the same class it dwarfs the number
  beside it — on real data "Printing" towered over "52%" on the printer above it.
  A word therefore sits one step down, which keeps the number the loudest thing
  on the screen. That is the right emphasis anyway: the number is the thing you
  read from across a room.
{% endcomment %}
{% case printers.size %}
  {% when 1 %}
    {% assign headline = "value--giga" %}
    {% assign headline_word = "value--xlarge" %}
  {% when 2 %}
    {% assign headline = "value--xlarge" %}
    {% assign headline_word = "value--large" %}
  {% else %}
    {% assign headline = "value--large" %}
    {% assign headline_word = "value--base" %}
{% endcase %}

<div class="layout layout--col layout--stretch">
  {% for printer in printers %}
    {% comment %}
      A `.list` per printer, not a `.layout`. The framework gives `.layout`
      `container-type: size`, so its own contents do not contribute to its
      height and a `.layout` nothing is stretching collapses to nothing.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% assign unreadable = false %}
    {% if printer.stale or printer.state == "offline" or printer.state == "unknown" %}
      {% assign unreadable = true %}
    {% endif %}

    {% comment %}
      The headline is a percentage only when there is progress to show. Without
      one it is the state itself, and repeating that state in the label below
      produced "Printing / Name - Printing" - which is what the hosted tier
      always looks like, because HTTP carries no progress. So the label names
      the state only when the headline is a number.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% assign headline_is_number = false %}
    {% if unreadable == false and printer.state != "idle" and printer.progress %}
      {% assign headline_is_number = true %}
    {% endif %}

    <div class="list">
      <div class="item">
        <div class="content">
          {% if printer.stale %}
            {% comment %}
              Deliberately no number. A stale reading is the bridge's fault
              rather than the printer's, and showing the last known figures at
              headline size would present them as current.
            {% endcomment %}
            <span class="value {{ headline_word | escape }}">Reading old</span>
          {% elsif printer.state == "offline" %}
            <span class="value {{ headline_word | escape }}">Offline</span>
          {% elsif printer.state == "unknown" %}
            <span class="value {{ headline_word | escape }}">Unknown</span>
          {% elsif printer.state == "idle" %}
            <span class="value {{ headline_word | escape }}">Ready</span>
          {% elsif printer.progress %}
            <span class="value {{ headline | escape }} value--tnums">{{ printer.progress | escape }}%</span>
          {% else %}
            <span class="value {{ headline_word | escape }}">{{ printer.state | capitalize | escape }}</span>
          {% endif %}

          <span class="label">
            {% if printer.name %}{{ printer.name | escape }}{% else %}Printer {{ forloop.index | escape }}{% endif %}
            {% if headline_is_number %}
              &middot; {{ printer.state | capitalize | escape }}
            {% endif %}
            {% unless unreadable %}
              {% comment %}
                Bambu's stage for a running print is frequently the word
                "Printing", which is the state as well, so the label read
                "Name - Printing - Printing" on real data. A stage only earns its
                place when it says something the state does not.
              {% endcomment %}
              {% assign state_word = printer.state | downcase %}
              {% assign stage_word = printer.stage | downcase %}
              {% if printer.stage and stage_word != state_word %}
                &middot; {{ printer.stage | escape }}
              {% endif %}
              {% if printer.job %} &middot; {{ printer.job | escape }}{% endif %}
              {% comment %}
                At three printers the figures move onto this line, because the
                grid below needs about 45px and three blocks of headline, label,
                rail and grid come to more than the 430px available. Losing the
                labels is a smaller cost than clipping the percentages.
              {% endcomment %}
              {% if printers.size > 2 %}
                {% if printer.remaining %} &middot; {{ printer.remaining | escape }} left{% endif %}
                {% if printer.layer %} &middot; layer {{ printer.layer | escape }}{% if printer.layers %}/{{ printer.layers | escape }}{% endif %}{% endif %}
              {% endif %}
              {% if printer.alert %} &middot; ALERT {{ printer.alert | escape }}{% endif %}
            {% endunless %}
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      {% comment %}
        Both the rail and the figures sit behind the same guard as the headline.
        The payload still carries a percentage for a printer that was printing
        when we last heard from it, so a filled rail beneath an unavailable
        state would show an old value as though it were current.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% unless unreadable %}
        {% render "progress_rail", progress: printer.progress, size: "progress-bar--large" %}
        {% unless printers.size > 2 %}
          {% render "printer_stats", printer: printer %}
        {% endunless %}
      {% endunless %}
    </div>
  {% endfor %}
</div>

{% render "plugin_footer", hidden: hidden, cloud: cloud, updated_at: updated_at %}
